// The render package: drawing lit 3D meshes on the GPU, through the gpu
// package (Vulkan underneath, and nothing of Vulkan's here). A program reaches
// it with 'import render', and gets:
//
// - Renderer, which owns what drawing needs: the pipelines of its materials,
//   the frame's uniforms, and the resources it hands out handles to --
//   meshes, textures, materials -- each kept in a generational pool (the
//   pool package), so a handle to something removed reaches nothing.
// - GpuMesh, a mesh.Mesh's vertex streams (positions, normals, uvs, each its
//   own vertex buffer, as the Mesh keeps them) and its 32-bit indices,
//   uploaded once into GPU buffers and kept; each of the mesh's material
//   groups is one draw. A MeshHandle names one, and its contents can be
//   replaced under the same handle ('replaceMesh'): the new version is
//   uploaded at once and drawn from the next frame on, and the old one's
//   buffers are released to the frame that last drew it, destroyed when the
//   GPU has finished it. That is what meshes that arrive at several levels
//   of detail, sculpted at run time, need. A dynamic mesh
//   ('addDynamicMesh') is room for a mesh rewritten in place frame by frame
//   ('writeDynamicMesh').
// - Giving back, for a world that is replaced by another: every handle is
//   released one by one, as 'removeMesh' does a mesh -- 'removeMaterial',
//   'removeTexture' (the textures a material was made with are the caller's
//   to remove: render keeps no list, and a texture may serve several
//   materials), 'RenderView.release' -- and the handle reaches nothing at
//   once. Nothing waits: the GPU memory is destroyed when the GPU has
//   finished the frames that may have used it (gpu's deletion queue,
//   'queue.releaseTexture'). Scene items and draws that still name a
//   removed material are skipped. 'resourceUse' counts what the renderer
//   holds (meshes, textures, materials, pipelines and their bytes).
//   Pipelines are not per world and have no release.
// - Custom pipelines (custom.cone): shaders of the caller's own
//   ('addPipeline'), importing renderframe.slang, whose materials
//   ('addCustomMaterial') bind parameters and textures of their own (an
//   r32float field from 'addFieldTexture', read in the vertex stage, for a
//   heightfield), drawn, batched and instanced with the rest.
// - LodChain, the levels of detail of one object, most detailed first
//   (LOD 0, as Unreal, Unity and Godot number them), chosen by index or by
//   the size the object is drawn at, with hysteresis so it does not flicker
//   at a threshold.
// - Camera (perspective or orthogonal, depth from 0 to 1, placed by origin
//   and orientation or 'lookAt') and Light (one directional light and
//   ambient light).
// - Material, the standard lit one: a base color times a texture (the
//   texture optional), Lambert diffuse and Blinn-Phong specular, and an
//   emissive color, in Slang (lit.slang); and lines in one flat color
//   (lines.slang), for wireframes and edges. Every material has an alpha
//   mode, glTF's: opaque, cut out (ALPHA_MASK: a fragment below the
//   cut-off is discarded; leaves, fences) or see-through (ALPHA_BLEND:
//   glass, glowing panes, light painted on with an alpha of 0).
// - One lighting convention for every material (renderframe.slang): the
//   light's color is irradiance (lux in physical units), ambient light,
//   sky and emissive colors are radiance (nits), and everything drawn is
//   multiplied by 'exposure' as it is written ('exposureFromEv100').
