pub extern fn buildMipsCut(rgba: &List[u8], width: u32, height: u32, kind: u32, cutoff: f32) List[u8];

//#line 1 "morph.cone"
// Morph targets (blend shapes): a mesh that changes shape by a weighted sum
// of stored displacements, drawn by the ordinary pipelines, shadows
// included.
//
// glTF 2.0's MORPH TARGETS. A target is a displacement of the mesh's vertices
// (of its positions, and optionally of its normals and tangents); a morphed
// vertex is its base value plus the sum, over the targets, of the target's
// weight times the target's displacement; a morphed normal is made unit
// length again. The weights are ordinary numbers, not limited to 0 to 1, and
// the order is MORPH FIRST, THEN SKIN: the morphed vertex is what the bones
// carry. Unreal's morph targets, Unity's blend shapes, Godot's blend shapes
// and ARKit's face shapes are this model.
//
// METHOD: A COMPUTE PRE-PASS WRITING A COPY'S OWN VERTEX BUFFERS. Three
// ways to apply the sum are in use, and this is the second:
//   - in the vertex stage (Filament, Godot 3): every pipeline variant (lit,
//     physically based, blended, skinned, the eight shadow casters, the
//     velocity pass) grows morph code and a binding for the targets; the
//     vertex stage does the work again for every pass that draws the copy
//     (the main pass, each shadow cascade, each local light's faces);
//   - in a compute pre-pass writing the deformed vertices to buffers
//     (Unreal, Unity on desktop, Godot 4): the sum is done once a frame, a
//     draw reads positions and normals as for any mesh, and nothing else in
//     the renderer knows about morphing;
//   - transform feedback, which Vulkan does not have.
// The compute pre-pass costs memory (each morphed COPY has its own positions
// and normals, 24 bytes a vertex, and its own draw call: copies of one
// morphed mesh do not batch, since they differ in shape) and a dispatch for
// a copy whose weights changed; it saves the changes to every pipeline,
// makes skinning follow morphing for free (the skinned vertex stage reads
// the morphed buffers), makes every shadow caster cast the morphed shape,
// and a draw of a morphed copy costs what a draw of any mesh costs, however
// many targets the mesh has. A copy whose weights did not change since the
// last frame costs no compute at all.
//
// THE PARTS.
//   MorphTargets   the displacements, on the CPU: N targets over a mesh's M
//                  vertices, each target sparse (only the vertices it moves)
//                  or dense, positions, normals and tangents. It is
//                  gltfimport's morph target data in this package's own type
//                  (render does not import gltfimport); 'addDense' takes
//                  the three lists a glTF primitive's target holds. It also
//                  holds the CPU reference sum ('position', 'normal',
//                  'tangent') the GPU's is checked against.
//   Morpher        what runs the pre-pass: 'addSet' uploads a MorphTargets
//                  for a base mesh; 'addCopy' makes a morphed copy of a mesh
//                  the renderer holds, a MeshHandle of its own; 'setWeights'
//                  gives the copy its weights, the non-zero ones kept as a
//                  list sorted by target; 'update' records the compute pass
//                  for the copies whose weights changed. Call 'update' on the
//                  frame's encoder before 'Renderer.render'.
//   MorphMixer     the face-rig layer above the weights, CPU only: named
//                  channels (visemes, blinks) driving targets, with
//                  corrective targets that fire on a product of channels.
//
// A morphed copy's MeshHandle is used wherever a mesh's is: 'DrawList.add' or
// 'addSkinned' (if the base mesh is skinned, the copy has its joints and
// weights and takes its bones), the lit and physically based materials,
// blended or not, the sun's cascades and the local lights' shadows. Its
// bounding sphere is the base's grown by the sum, over the targets, of the
// weight's magnitude times the target's largest displacement, kept current by
// 'setWeights'. Remove a copy with 'removeCopy' here, not with
// 'removeMesh' (its compute work and bind group name its buffers).
//
// A COPY OWNS ALL ITS BUFFERS. A GPU buffer has one owner in this renderer
// (a GpuMesh holds its buffers, and a mesh's release destroys them), so a copy
// cannot borrow the base's index, uv, joint and weight buffers: 'addCopy'
// copies them on the GPU, once. That is the memory cost of this design for
// many copies of one mesh (a crowd of 100 faces of 10,000 vertices: about 60
// MB of vertex data in all); a base mesh may be replaced or removed once a
// copy exists. Sharing the unchanging streams between copies would be a change
// to GpuMesh (a reference-counted buffer), and is the first thing to do if a
// scene wants hundreds of morphing copies of one mesh.
//
// MEMORY AND LIMITS. A set holds per entry (a vertex a target moves) 4 bytes of
// target number and 24 of displacement, per vertex 4 of row pointer and 24 of
// the base mesh's positions and normals; a copy holds a mesh's buffers and 8
// bytes a target. 64 targets of 10,000 vertices, dense, is 18 MB; sparse, less.
// Any number of targets above 0 works (the active list holds as many as the
// set has targets); the largest set is the 128 MB a storage buffer binds
// (about 5 million entries). The tangent displacements glTF carries are kept
// in MorphTargets and used by its CPU reference but not uploaded: render has
// no tangent stream, finding tangents from screen-space derivatives.
//
// THE VELOCITY BUFFER. A temporal anti-aliasing pass wants each pixel's motion,
// including a morph's. 'addCopy' with 'keepPrevious' keeps the copy's
// positions of the frame before ('previousPositions'): a vertex buffer of the
// same layout as the copy's own, refreshed by 'update', so that a velocity
// pass can bind it as a second position stream and draw the difference of the
// two clip positions. It costs 12 more bytes a vertex and a buffer copy on a
// frame whose weights changed.
//
// A FACE RIG ON THIS. See MorphMixer: visemes and blinks are targets of the
// mesh, each modelled as a displacement from the neutral face; a viewer
// drives CHANNELS (jaw open, a viseme, blink left), each channel setting a
// weight of one or more targets (ARKit's 52 blend shapes are channels with
// one target each); targets are ADDITIVE, so a blink and a viseme sum; where
// two shapes together look wrong (a smile with the jaw wide open) a
// CORRECTIVE target, modelled as the difference between the true combined
// shape and the sum of the two, takes the product of the two channels as its
// weight, so it is on only as much as both are. Weights are computed on the
// CPU in the frame, from a keyframetracks clip's PROP_WEIGHTS track (or lip
