// THE METHOD, named (the choices are listed in Design\render.md):
// - Karis, "High Quality Temporal Supersampling", SIGGRAPH 2014 (Unreal 4):
//   the projection jittered by the Halton (2, 3) sequence, the history
//   reprojected, clipped to the current frame's neighbourhood, and blended.
// - Salvi, "An Excursion in Temporal Supersampling", GDC 2016: the
//   neighbourhood clip by mean and variance in YCoCg.
// - Pedersen, "Temporal Reprojection Anti-Aliasing in INSIDE", GDC 2016: the
//   closest-depth velocity of the 3 x 3, the clip towards the box's centre.
// - Jimenez, "Filmic SMAA", SIGGRAPH 2016: the Catmull-Rom history filter in
//   five taps.
// - Lottes, FidelityFX CAS, 2019: the contrast-adaptive sharpening.
//
// WHAT MOVES. The velocity of a pixel is, for a static object, the camera's
// motion alone, which the resolve finds from the depth and last frame's
// camera matrix; nothing is drawn for it. An object that moves by itself, or
// a skinned one that is posed differently, is named to the pass by the
// program in a MotionList (the item's index in the draw list, last frame's
// model matrix, last frame's pose), and drawn again into a velocity texture
