// ---- Drawing into a headset ----
//
// 'Headset' puts the renderer's pictures into a headset through OpenXR (the
// openxr package), by XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2: the runtime makes the Vulkan
// instance and device, and hands over its swapchains' images as VkImages,
// which the gpu package wraps as textures ('Device.wrapImage') that the
// renderer draws into as it draws into any texture. The runtime does the rest:
// lens distortion, reprojection, and the compositing. A program that draws to
// a window never reaches this file, and its pictures are as they were: nor
// does it ask the OS for the OpenXR loader (the openxr package opens it when a
// headset is first opened, not when the program starts).
//
// THE GPU is the program's, not the headset's. 'Headset.open' makes the
// runtime's Vulkan instance, the adapter it chose and the device on it, and
// puts them in a 'HeadsetGpu' the caller made first, so that the program owns
// the device its renderer is made on and can end everything in the order
// Vulkan needs: the renderer and what was made from the device, then the
// headset (OpenXR's session, swapchains and spaces; the eye images' textures),
// then the device, the adapter and the instance. Declare the 'HeadsetGpu'
// before the headset and the renderer after it, and a function's locals end in
// that order. Every call that records or submits is handed the device.
//
//   var gpu = HeadsetGpu.none();
//   var headset = Headset.open("my app", &HeadsetOptions(), &mut gpu);
//   if !headset.isValid() { ...no OpenXR runtime, or no headset: say so... }
//   var renderer = Renderer.create(&mut gpu.device, headset.colorFormat, headset.depthFormat);
//   ... add meshes and materials to the renderer, through &mut gpu.device ...
//   while headset.pollEvents() {                      // false once the session is over
//     let frame = headset.beginFrame(&mut gpu.device);  // waits for the runtime's frame, locates the eyes
//     if frame.shouldRender {
//       var eye = 0usize;
//       while eye < 2usize {
//         let camera = headset.eyeCamera(&frame, eye);     // placed at the eye, its own field of view
//         headset.drawEye(&mut gpu.device, &frame, eye, &mut renderer, &camera, &light, &list);
//         eye = eye + 1usize;
//       }
//     }
//     headset.endFrame(&mut gpu.device, &frame);      // submits the eyes as a projection layer
//   }
//   gpu.device.waitIdle();
//
// THE FRAME LOOP is the runtime's (OpenXR's xrWaitFrame / xrBeginFrame /
// xrEndFrame): 'beginFrame' waits until the runtime wants the frame, which is
// what holds the program to the headset's rate, and answers the display time
// the frame is predicted to be shown at and the eyes' poses at that time
// ('xrLocateViews'); 'drawEye' draws one eye and 'endFrame' hands both to the
// runtime. A frame the runtime does not want drawn ('shouldRender' false: the
// headset is off the head, say) is begun and ended with no layer. 'pollEvents'
// follows the session's life: when the runtime says READY it begins the
// session, when it says STOPPING it ends it, and when the session is over
// (EXITING, LOSS_PENDING, or the instance is lost) it answers false. A
// program that wants to leave asks 'requestExit' and keeps calling
// 'pollEvents' until it answers false.
//
// THE EYES are drawn one after the other, each into an image of its own
// swapchain, with the renderer's ordinary path: acquire the image, wait for
// it, 'render', submit, release. Each eye is its own submission, because the
// renderer's frame uniforms are written ahead of the next submission, so two
// eyes in one would share a camera. (Multiview, both eyes in one pass, is a
// later optimisation, and not built.) The camera of an eye is off-axis: the
// runtime's four field-of-view angles are the edges of its projection
// ('Camera.setFovAngles'), the projection is the renderer's own convention
// (depth from 0 at the near plane to 1 at the far, not reversed, 'near' 0.1
// and 'far' 1000 unless the options say otherwise), and the pose is the
// eye's, in the reference space ('referenceSpace': local floor where the
// runtime has it, so y = 0 is the floor, else local). A program places its
// player in the world by following: 'camera.following' with 'followMatrix' the
// player's placement ('Camera.placement').
//
// A program with a pipeline of its own (a render system with passes before and
// after 'render') draws an eye with the pieces 'drawEye' is made of:
// 'acquireEye', then 'swapEye' to put the eye's color and depth textures into
// a 'SurfaceTexture' the program draws into as it draws into a window's, with
// 'eyeStart' and 'eyeEnd' round the pass in its command encoder (the eye's
// timestamps, and the capture), 'eyeSubmitted' after the submit, 'swapEye'
// again to give the textures back, and 'releaseEye' once everything that
// uses the image has been submitted.
//
// THE MIRROR. A program that shows a window while the headset runs asks for it
// in the options ('mirrorWindow', the window's SDL handle as a number, and its
// size). The instance and device then carry what a window's surface and
// swapchain need (the runtime lets the program add its own extensions to the
// ones it asks for), and 'mirrorEye' blits the middle of an eye's image,
// cropped to the window's shape, into the window's swapchain and presents it.
// The mirror is presented without waiting for the display and skips a frame
// when the window has no free image, so the headset never runs at the window's
// rate. Call 'mirrorEye' after the eye is drawn and before it is released.
//
// THE COST of each eye is measured on both sides: the CPU's time to record and
// submit it ('cpuMs') and the GPU's time for its pass, between two timestamps
// ('gpuMs'), read back a few frames later when the GPU is done with them.
//
// THE CONTROLLERS are read once a frame by 'beginFrame', so a program never
// touches OpenXR for them. 'Headset.open' makes one action set, "gameplay",
// with a grip pose and an aim pose, trigger and squeeze values, a thumbstick
// and its click, a primary (A, X) and a secondary (B, Y) button, a menu button
// and a vibration output, each for the left hand and the right; suggests their
// bindings for Meta's Touch controllers (the Quest 3's pair too) and, as a
// fallback, the Khronos simple controller (select is the trigger); and
// attaches the set to the session. Each frame, once the session is FOCUSED
// (a runtime gives a session that is not no input), it syncs the actions,
// reads them and locates both poses of both hands in the eyes' reference space
// at the frame's predicted display time. The result is 'frame.hands', or
// 'headset.hand(&frame, 0)' (0 left, 1 right): a plain 'HeadsetHand' with the
// poses as 'Vec3' and 'Quat' like the eyes', and each button as down-now and
// pressed or released since the previous frame ('HeadsetButton'), so a program
// needs no history of its own for a click or a snap turn. 'headset.vibrate'
// buzzes a controller. With no controllers, no profile, no input in the
// runtime, or a session not focused, every hand is inactive (its values at
// rest) and nothing fails. 'profileName' says which interaction profile the
// runtime bound to a hand, as it last said ('pollEvents' follows the change).
//
//   let left = headset.hand(&frame, 0usize);
//   if left.active && left.primary.pressed { ... }
//   if left.stickX > 0.7 && !turned { ...snap turn... }
//
// What it does not do yet: hand and eye tracking and other vendor input
// (extensions), the controllers' own models, the depth layer, foveation, the headset's refresh-rate control, a
// session that ends and begins again, and comfort (vignetting, a steady
// horizon, snap turns), which are the program's.

// What 'Headset.open' is asked for
pub struct HeadsetOptions {
  // The Khronos validation layer on the Vulkan instance, where it is
  // installed. Off by default: Meta's PC simulator leaks memory under it.
  pub validation: bool = false;
  // The eye images can be copied from, so 'captureEye' can read one back
  // (they are made with the transfer-source usage)
  pub copySource: bool = false;
  // The near and far planes of every eye's camera, in metres
  pub near: f32 = 0.1;
  pub far: f32 = 1000.;
  // A window to mirror the left eye into: its SDL handle as a number (window's
  // 'sdlHandle'), and the size of its drawable area in pixels. 0: none.
  pub mirrorWindow: usize = 0usize;
  pub mirrorWidth: u32 = 0u32;
  pub mirrorHeight: u32 = 0u32;
  // Ask for VK_KHR_fragment_shading_rate's attachment rates on the headset's device (foveation.cone), if the GPU
  // offers them. Not offered, or refused by the runtime's device creation, the device is made without, one line
  // says so, and 'Headset.foveated' is false.
  pub foveation: bool = false;
  // Ask for ray queries and acceleration structures (VK_KHR_ray_query, VK_KHR_acceleration_structure) on the headset's device,
